From: Gran Turismo 6 for Mac <https://github.com/Wooloomooloo2/Gran-Turismo-6-Mac>
Subject: [PATCH] cellSpurs: the workload flag, InitializeWithAttribute2, DestroyTaskset2

Upstream: not yet submitted; written to be upstreamed (generic, no title names)
Licence: MIT, as ps3recomp (see its LICENSE); offered under the same terms
Base: ps3recomp 4e8f3ca87a3e1d87bb83b8b7e9348fe2685cd514 (after 0001-0012)

What: four cellSpurs imports had no handler, so they took the unresolved path
and faked CELL_OK:
- _cellSpursWorkloadFlagReceiver2 (0x2DDBCC0A) and cellSpursGetWorkloadFlag
  (0xC765B995), the workload flag. A title names one workload of an instance
  the receiver (instance byte 0x77), gets the address of the instance's
  CellSpursWorkloadFlag (+0x60; the flag word is at +0x6C) and stores 0 into
  the flag to ask the kernel to run the receiver. The kernel now does that:
  a receiver whose flag is 0 is kicked like a ready count, and once selected
  the flag goes back to all ones (a plain big-endian store under the lock-line
  lock: vm_write32 can take that lock again when an SPU holds a reservation
  on the line, which deadlocked the kernel). Initialise sets the flag to all ones and the
  receiver to 0xFF (it wrote all ones over +0x60..+0x67, which are unused,
  and left the flag word and receiver 0). Setting a receiver while one is set
  is BUSY, clearing one that is not the caller's PERM; a wid past the
  instance's workloads is INVAL, one not added SRCH, a pending exception STAT,
  NULL and misalignment NULL_POINTER and ALIGN (the policy-module codes,
  0x804108xx). _cellSpursWorkloadFlagReceiver (the first version, which also
  pulls the flag to all ones) is added with it.
- cellSpursInitializeWithAttribute2 (0x30AA96C4): a CellSpurs2. It initialises
  the instance as cellSpursInitializeWithAttribute does, clearing all 8 KB and
  setting flags1 SF1_32_WORKLOADS, after the NULL and alignment checks.
  Without it the instance was never initialised and its kernel never ran.
- cellSpursDestroyTaskset2 (0x1EBCF459): destroys as cellSpursDestroyTaskset,
  after the NULL and 128-byte alignment checks.
Behaviour and error codes follow RPCS3 (cellSpurs and its SPU kernel). The
runtime still has 16 workload slots; a CellSpurs2's workloads 16-31 cannot be
added.

Why: the faked GetWorkloadFlag left the caller's pointer unwritten, so its
store to "the flag" went wherever that pointer pointed and the receiver was
never asked to run; a faked InitializeWithAttribute2 left a SPURS instance
with no kernel.

Test: tools/test_spurs_workload_flag.py (new) checks the four names are
registered under the imported NIDs, then builds tests/test_spurs_workload_flag.c
with the real cellSpurs.c and the SPU runtime, with a manufactured policy
module: every error code above, the receiver set and cleared, the flag
address, a flag stored 0 taken by the kernel (the receiver runs and the flag
reads all ones again, with the policy module holding a reservation on the
line), the CellSpurs2 cleared with flags1 set and a live
kernel, and DestroyTaskset2.

